# Gmail OAuth token refresh runbook

The Gmail ingestion agent authenticates with a Google OAuth **refresh token**. The
consent screen for Google Cloud project `jobtracker-504817` is in **Testing** mode,
and Testing-mode refresh tokens expire **7 days** after they are minted — regardless
of activity or test-user status. When that happens the hourly `GmailAgentFunction`
run starts failing with:

```
invalid_grant: Token has been expired or revoked.
```

Publishing the consent screen to Production would remove the expiry, but the
restricted `gmail.readonly` scope would then require Google's CASA security
assessment — not worth it for a project meant to be shut down once the job search
ends. So this is a recurring ~weekly manual chore. See open follow-up #4 in
`jobtracker-status.md`.

## How you find out

- **In-app notification bell** on `https://jobtracker.ashleycjones.com` — bright
  amber with a red dot when the last run failed or nothing has succeeded in 3 hours.
  Click it for the error text.
- **Email alert** from the `GmailAgentErrorAlarm` CloudWatch alarm via the
  `IngestionAlertTopic` SNS topic (requires the one-time SNS subscription
  confirmation link to have been clicked).

## Procedure

All commands run from the repo root unless noted. `.env` (gitignored) is the source
of truth for every credential below.

### 1. Confirm the token is actually expired

Rules out a code/scheduling problem before re-minting anything:

```bash
set -a && source .env && set +a
curl -s -X POST https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token \
  -d client_id="$GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID" \
  -d client_secret="$GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET" \
  -d refresh_token="$GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN" \
  -d grant_type=refresh_token
```

`invalid_grant: Token has been expired or revoked.` confirms it. A `200` with an
`access_token` means the token is fine and the failure is something else.

### 2. Check the AWS session

The deploy in step 5 needs valid credentials:

```bash
aws sts get-caller-identity --profile jobtracker
```

If this reports an expired session, run `aws login --profile ashley-dev` yourself in
a terminal (browser-based flow). The `jobtracker` profile assumes a role from that
session and re-authenticates automatically once the base session is refreshed.

### 3. Mint a new refresh token

Needs a real browser, so run it directly in a terminal (in Claude Code, prefix with
`!` so it runs in your own session):

```bash
uv run backend/scripts/gmail_auth.py
```

Complete the Google consent screen. The script prints the new refresh token.

### 4. Update `.env`

Paste the printed value into `.env` as `GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN`. Re-run the curl from
step 1 to confirm it now returns `200` with `"scope":
"https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.readonly"`.

### 5. Build and deploy

The token lives in the `jobtracker-gmail-agent` secret, which is a CloudFormation
resource fed by a `NoEcho` parameter — so it only updates via a deploy.

```bash
cd infra
AWS_PROFILE=jobtracker ~/.venvs/sam-cli/bin/sam build --use-container
set -a && source ../.env && set +a
AWS_PROFILE=jobtracker ~/.venvs/sam-cli/bin/sam deploy \
  --stack-name jobtracker \
  --s3-bucket "jobtracker-sam-artifacts-${AWS_ACCOUNT_ID}" \
  --s3-prefix jobtracker \
  --capabilities CAPABILITY_IAM \
  --region us-east-1 \
  --parameter-overrides \
    FrontendOrigin=https://jobtracker.ashleycjones.com \
    GoogleClientId="$GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID" \
    GoogleClientSecret="$GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET" \
    GoogleRefreshToken="$GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN" \
    OpenAiApiKey="$OPENAI_API_KEY" \
    AlertEmail="$ALERT_EMAIL" \
  --no-confirm-changeset --no-fail-on-empty-changeset
```

Notes:
- Always `sam build` before `sam deploy`. Deploying without a fresh build silently
  ships the stale `.aws-sam/build/template.yaml`.
- `SessionSecret` is deliberately omitted — omitting a parameter on an existing
  stack preserves its current value, and the secret isn't recorded anywhere.
- Only `GmailAgentSecret` should change if nothing else in the tree was edited.

### 6. Verify

```bash
FN=$(AWS_PROFILE=jobtracker aws cloudformation describe-stack-resource \
  --stack-name jobtracker --logical-resource-id GmailAgentFunction \
  --region us-east-1 --query 'StackResourceDetail.PhysicalResourceId' --output text)

AWS_PROFILE=jobtracker aws lambda invoke --function-name "$FN" \
  --region us-east-1 /tmp/invoke.json && cat /tmp/invoke.json
```

Expect `{"found": N, "skipped": M}` with no error. `found: 0` is normal when the
outage was short — everything in the 2-day recency window had already been ingested.
A large `found` is normal after a long outage, up to the window limit.

Then confirm the status record the bell reads is healthy:

```bash
AWS_PROFILE=jobtracker aws dynamodb get-item \
  --table-name JobTrackerTable --region us-east-1 \
  --key '{"job_id":{"S":"config:ingestion_status"}}'
```

Expect `success: true` and a `last_run_at` of just now.

Note the DynamoDB key schema is a single `job_id` partition key — config rows are
`config:<name>`, not a `pk`/`sk` pair.

## Gotchas

- **`aws lambda invoke` can double-invoke on a slow cold start.** The CLI may retry
  mid-flight during a ~16s cold start and return the second call's response. Both
  really run. Harmless — `gmail_agent.py` dedupes against DynamoDB before writing.
- **The CloudWatch alarm may sit in `ALARM` right after a fix**, because its 1-hour
  lookback still includes the pre-fix failures. It self-clears after a full
  error-free hour.

## Log of past refreshes

| Date | Trigger | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 2026-08-28 | Silent outage since 2026-08-15, found manually 13 days later | `{"found": 33, "skipped": 26}` — backlog cleared. Alerting added the same day so it can't go unnoticed again. |
| 2026-09-04 | Routine refresh | `{"found": 0, "skipped": 27}` — 27 emails in window, all already tracked, no new jobs. |
